course: dictionary
lesson: 7639
title: Using the word professor
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A professor is a senior teacher at a university, and usually an expert in one subject. «Professor Smith teaches maths at the university.» Here Professor is a title, like Doctor, and it tells you the person is a respected expert.
explain: In stories, «the professor» is often a clever scientist who may be absent-minded, forgetting things like his keys or where he left his glasses. Clever is the clue: the professor knows a lot, even if he forgets small things.
explain: Now read the chat in Jake's group. Sam was added by mistake, and the others call him «Professor Sam» as a joke. The words «no nerds allowed» are the context clue: the title is teasing him for seeming like a know-it-all, not for teaching at a university.
fact: A professor is a senior teacher at a university, often an expert in one subject.
fact: In the US, «professor» can mean any university teacher.
fact: In stories, «the professor» is often a clever, sometimes absent-minded scientist.
fact: Phrases you may see are «professor of maths» and «absent-minded professor».
fact: A claim is true or false all by itself: whether a professor, a six-year-old or your annoying cousin says it does not change the truth.
exercise: In «Professor Smith teaches maths at the university», what is a professor? | A shop that sells maths books | A pupil who is good at sums | *A senior teacher at a university, often an expert in one subject | A kind of school bus driver
exercise: In stories, «the professor» is often what kind of character? | A rude shopkeeper who never smiles | *A clever, sometimes absent-minded scientist | A dog who wins a race | A baby who cannot talk yet
exercise: Jake's mates add Sam to a «no nerds allowed» chat and call him «Professor Sam». Why? | Because he has won a prize for swimming | Because he has forgotten his name | Because he is a real university teacher | *As a joke, because they think he acts like a know-it-all
test: «Whether a professor says it, a six-year-old says it, or your cousin says it, two plus two is four.» What does this tell you about a claim? | *It is true or false all by itself, whoever says it | It changes depending on who says it | It is only true when a professor says it | It is always false when a child says it
test: In the US, what can «professor» mean? | A pupil who is top of the class | Only the highest rank in a school | A bus driver | *Any university teacher
exam: Which phrase uses «professor» in the way the dictionary describes? | a box of crayons | a sunny afternoon at the beach | a loud red bus | *professor of maths
